  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Sunday)...\n\nDene Fog Advisory will remain in effect through 10 AM this morning\nfor our western foothill zones. Observation systems indicate areas\nof significantly reduced visibility and low stratus. Models\ncontinue to suggest boundary layer will remain at or near \nsaturation in this area overnight into the morning. Conditions \nshould improve thru the day as winds become westerly/downslope. \nFog potential will shift to the east and be much lower Monday \nnight as downslope gradient influence prevails. \n\nA weak upper trough will track over the region today. Most of the\nenergy is still expected to pass well to our south. Weak ascent \nmay produce some scattered light snow showers over our southern \nmountains. Lower elevations will stay dry. Temperatures today \nwill be a bit above yesterdays, but still cooler than normal in \nthe lower to mid 30s. \n\nWe may see some snow showers over the high country by Thursday and\nFriday as heights fall a bit and Pacific moisture works through\nthe Rockies on westerly flow. However, much of the rest of the \nwork week looks dry with warming temps due to downslope flow.\nHighs will reach the 50s for latter part of the week. This will \nlead to lee side surface troughing and favorable surface pressure \npatterns for increased wind in and near our foothills, especially \ngap locations (Livingston, Big Timber, Harlowton). We may see \ngusts 40-50 mph in these areas by daybreak Tuesday (Probabilities \nfor 50+ mph is 40%). After some decrease during the daytime \nTuesday, expect the gradient to tighten again Tuesday night into \nearly Wednesday. \n\nThe strongest wind in these areas may come Thursday into Friday. \nIncreasing mid level winds (near 50 knots at 700mb) and tightening\nsurface gradients (15-20mb from IDA-LWT per the models) suggest a\nhigh probability of 50+ mph gusts (80-90%) and a good chance of \n60+ mph gusts (45-65% chance). This may affect holiday travelers \nin high profile vehicles, so something to keep an eye on.\n\nModels are trending more consistently with a trough through NE MT \non Friday. This would lead to cold advection and gusty/mixed W-NW\nwinds spreading across lower elevations. There is considerable \nensemble spread in the strength of this wave and magnitude of \npost-frontal winds, and we will monitor this closely. Currently,\nsuggest a 40-70% chance of wind gusts over 40 mph across our\neastern plains Friday. \n\nEnsembles indicate NW flow over our area for the weekend with some\nmodest cooling. We are anticipating highs mainly in the 40s.\nScattered rain/snow showers would still be possible over the high\ncountry and higher foothills. \n\nHoliday Travel...We are entering a holiday week and the days \nleading up to Thanksgiving look dry, and travel conditions around \nthe region should be good. Drivers of high profile vehicles should \nkeep an eye on wind forecasts. Mountain passes could be impacted \nby snow showers at times Thursday night thru the weekend. BT",
